## 21. DEFINITION OF DONE

The product is not complete until these end-to-end scenarios work:

SCENARIO A — SHOP ONBOARDING
A YBGP administrator can provision a business. The owner can configure its profile, links, Google Reviews destination, and loyalty rules. The system generates a stable public QR.

SCENARIO B — CUSTOMER REGISTRATION
A new customer scans the shop QR, registers with their name and mobile number, completes configured verification, and receives a private digital stamp card.

SCENARIO C — AWARD STAMP
An authenticated cashier opens the scanner, scans the customer's card, sees the correct customer and progress, confirms the award, and receives a server-confirmed success response.

SCENARIO D — REWARD
When the threshold is reached, the correct reward becomes available. The cashier redeems it exactly once, and the customer sees the updated status.

SCENARIO E — TENANT SECURITY
A cashier from one business cannot access another business's customers, transactions, rewards, or administrative settings.

SCENARIO F — YBGP CONTROL
An authorized platform administrator can inspect business status, subscription entitlements, platform usage, and audit records without exposing customer secrets.

SCENARIO G — FAILURE RECOVERY
Invalid QR tokens, network failures, repeated requests, expired cards, missing permissions, and failed database operations produce safe, understandable responses without corrupting balances.

SCENARIO H — DEPLOYMENT
A clean environment can be initialized, migrated, seeded with safe demo data, tested, and deployed using the documented procedure.

